  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/C.A.Victor vsFoodInspector on 8 September, 2020.json",
  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "C.A.Victor vs Food Inspector on 8 September, 2020 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr.Justice N.Anil Kumar Tuesday, The 08Th Day Of September 2020 / 17Th Bhadra, 1942 Crl.Rev.Pet.No.983 Of 2001 Cra 39/1996 Of Additional Sessions Court, North Paravur Cc 84/1992 Of Judicial Magistrate Of First Class-I, Aluva Revision Petitioner/S: C.A.Victor, S/O Antony, Chirakkamanavalan House, Airoor, Kunnukara, Alwaye By Advs. Sri.T.M.Abdul Latheef Sri.Santhosh Subramanian Sri.Sheji P.Abraham Sri.Sherry J. Thomas Respondent/S: 1 Food Inspector, Angamaly Circle, Angamaly 2 State Of Kerala, Represented By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam By Public Prosecutor Smt.M.N.Maya This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 17-08-2020, The Court On 08-09-2020 Passed The Following: Crl.R.P. No.983/2001 ..2.. C.A.Victor vs Food Inspector on 8 September, 2020",
    "facts": "The revision petitioner is the accused in CC No.84/1992 on the file of the Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-I, Aluva. The 1st respondent filed a complaint before the learned magistrate alleging offences punishable under Sections 2(ia)(a)(b)(c)(j) (l), 7(i) and (v) r/w Section 16(1-A)(i) and Section 7(iii) and (v) r/w Section 16(1)(a) (ii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act (hereinafter referred to as \"PFA Act\") and Rules 32 and 50 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955 (hereinafter referred to as \"PFA Rules\"). Feeling aggrieved, the accused is before this Court in revision. 4. The case of the complainant is that on ..4.. 22.11.1991 at 2 pm, he inspected the bakery of the accused functioning in Door No.VIII/458 in Parakkadavu Panchayath under the name and style, \"Gulf Bakery\", and purchased 600 gm of \"green peas fry\" and sampled it in accordance with law. One part of the sample was sent to the Public Analyst. After analysis, the Public Analyst reported that the sample is adulterated. It is further alleged that the green peas fry packets do not contain the labels under the PFA Act and the accused has no valid licence for the sale of green peas fry. C.A.Victor vs Food Inspector on 8 September, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/31196240/ 2 It is on the basis of Ext.P11 report, PW1 filed the complaint before the court. Ext.P11 ..5.. report dated 03.01.1992 by the Public Analyst would show that the sample of green peas fry contains 22.7% of insect damaged grains and is therefore, adulterated. 7. Subsequently, the second part of the sample was forwarded to the Central Food Laboratory. After analysis, the Central Food Laboratory forwarded Ext.P12 report stating that the sample contains total dye content more than 200 mg/kg and does not conform to Rule 30 of the PFA Rules, 1955. 8. It is settled principle of law that without getting a report of the Public Analyst, the Food Inspector cannot lodge a complaint alleging that there is misbranding.",
    "arguments": "In Johnson v. State of Kerala [2009 (4) KLT 202], this Court held that when the sample was collected by the Food Inspector in exercise of the power under Section 10 of the PFA Act following the procedure provided under Section 11 of the PFA ..6.. Act, it is mandatory that one of the three samples should be sent to the Public Analyst for examination and the two remaining samples are to be forwarded to the Local Health Authority. Prosecution for the offence under the PFA Act, in such circumstance, could only be lodged after getting the report of the Public Analyst as provided under Section 13 of the PFA Act. 9. In Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector [2010 (4) KHC 767] and Rasheed NK v. Food Inspector, Thalassery and Another (2016 (2) KHC 679], it was held that all the prosecutions under the PFA Act could be stifled by raising a contention that the \"laboratory\" or \"the methods of analysis\" was not defined, owing to the failure to define \"laboratory\" in terms of Section 23(1A)(ee) of the PFA Act, except in cases, where the article in food was lifted and sent for ..7.. analysis prior to the introduction of the provisions under Section 23(1A)(ee) of the PFA Act, i.e., with effect from 01.04.1976 and in cases such as the accused sold the food items without obtaining requisite licence or the duties of the Food Inspector was obstructed during inspection etc.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In Johnson v. State of Kerala [2009 (4) KLT 202], this Court held that when the sample was collected by the Food Inspector in exercise of the power under Section 10 of the PFA Act following the procedure provided under Section 11 of the PFA ..6.. Act, it is mandatory that one of the three samples should be sent to the Public Analyst for examination and the two remaining samples are to be forwarded to the Local Health Authority. Prosecution for the offence under the PFA Act, in such circumstance, could only be lodged after getting the report of the Public Analyst as provided under Section 13 of the PFA Act. 9. In Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector [2010 (4) KHC 767] and Rasheed NK v. Food Inspector, Thalassery and Another (2016 (2) KHC 679], it was held that all the prosecutions under the PFA Act could be stifled by raising a contention that the \"laboratory\" or \"the methods of analysis\" was not defined, owing to the failure to define \"laboratory\" in terms of Section 23(1A)(ee) of the PFA Act, except in cases, where the article in food was lifted and sent for ..7.. analysis prior to the introduction of the provisions under Section 23(1A)(ee) of the PFA Act, i.e., with effect from 01.04.1976 and in cases such as the accused sold the food items without obtaining requisite licence or the duties of the Food Inspector was obstructed during inspection etc."
  },
